  <subsection xml:id="subsec-weighted-graphs-shortest-paths">
    <title>Weighted Graphs and Shortest Paths</title>
    <p>In a route-finding problem, the weights may measure distance, travel time, or cost.
  Read the weights rather than measuring the lengths of the drawn edges.</p>
    <definition xml:id="def-total-path-weight">
      <title>Total Weight of a Path</title>
      <statement>
        <p>How do you combine the weights of the edges along a path? What units should the result have?</p>
        <p>
          
        </p>
        <p>
          
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <definition xml:id="def-shortest-path">
      <title>Shortest Path</title>
      <statement>
        <p>What quantity is minimized? Does a shortest path always use the fewest edges?</p>
        <p>
          
        </p>
        <p>
          
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <example xml:id="ex-comparing-campus-routes">
      <title>Comparing Campus Routes</title>
      <statement>
        <p>A student walks from location <m>A</m> to location <m>G</m>. Each edge weight is a walking time in minutes.
    Each walkway can be used in either direction.</p>
